# Owners for the paths no CI check can read.
#
# Everything below reaches production — the terraform that defines the cluster
# and the load balancer, the manifests the deploy applies, the workflows that do
# the applying, and the hooks that format every commit. Some of it is now
# checked: k8s-ci.yaml validates the manifests against the schema cow-cluster
# runs, repo-ci.yaml shellchecks the hooks and runs actionlint over every
# workflow, and infra-ci.yaml validates the terraform. None of that reads intent.
# A `paths:` filter narrowed by mistake is valid YAML that lints clean and leaves
# no run behind to notice; a URL map that parses and routes `/api/` to the web
# bucket validates just as cleanly. A human reading the diff is the only check
# those have, so this file makes GitHub ask for one and mark the files as owned
# in the review view.
#
# `.github/` includes this file. Left unowned, the way to skip a review on
# infra/terraform is to edit CODEOWNERS in the same pull request.
#
# One caveat, because it will otherwise be discovered the hard way: @skylerberg
# is the sole maintainer, and GitHub does not let anyone approve their own pull
# request. Turning on "Require review from Code Owners" in branch protection
# would therefore block every pull request he opens — which is all of them. Left
# as it is, CODEOWNERS still requests his review automatically and labels the
# owned files in the diff, which is the part that is useful today. Turn the
# branch-protection setting on when there is a second maintainer, not before.
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style and the LAST matching line wins, so anything added
# here has to go below a broader pattern it means to override. There is
# deliberately no `*` default-owner line: it would claim every pull request and
# make the signal on these four paths worthless. GitHub reports parse errors for
# this file under Settings → Code owners errors — a pattern that matches nothing
# is silently ignored, so check there after editing.

/.github/         @skylerberg
/.githooks/       @skylerberg
# The whole directory, not `/infra/terraform/`: anything that lands under
# `infra/` later is repo-wide infrastructure by the fact of being there, and an
# entry naming one subdirectory would leave it unowned until someone noticed.
/infra/           @skylerberg
/api/k8s/         @skylerberg

# Not infrastructure, but the same problem: the two-commit deploy rule and the
# no-root-workspace rule live here and nowhere else, and nothing enforces either.
/CLAUDE.md        @skylerberg
